> Hence why the default constructor exists as it is. It is very obvious when you have a bug at startup

This patch is not specific to "startup". It's about object construction which can happen at any point during runtime.

> aserver is all generated code, and worked before this patch, so clearly wasn't using this codepath.

The generated code fails quite easily because of this problem.

Try modifying a testcase to let a client query a property containing an invalid default constructed object path. Below diff can be used.

```
--- a/test/gen/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path.cpp
+++ b/test/gen/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path.cpp
@@ -1,21 +1,40 @@
+#include "client.hpp"
 #include "sdbusplus/async.hpp"
 #include "server/Test/aserver.hpp"

 #include <sdbusplus/async/context.hpp>

 #include <gtest/gtest.h>
+#include <iostream>

 class A : public sdbusplus::aserver::server::Test<A>
 {};

+sdbusplus::async::task<> mytask(sdbusplus::async::context& ctx){
+
+    sdbusplus::message::object_path path("/");
+
+    sdbusplus::aserver::server::Test<A> t2(ctx, path);
+
+    ctx.request_name("xyz.openbmc_project.Test");
+
+    auto client = sdbusplus::client::server::Test(ctx)
+    .path(path.str).service("xyz.openbmc_project.Test");
+
+    auto p = co_await client.object_path();
+    std::cout << p.str << std::endl;
+    ctx.request_stop();
+    co_return;
+}
+
 // Test that we can construct with an object path
 TEST(AServerConstructWithObjectPath, NoProps)
 {
     sdbusplus::async::context ctx;

-    sdbusplus::message::object_path path("/");
+    ctx.spawn(mytask(ctx));

-    sdbusplus::aserver::server::Test<A> t2(ctx, path);
+    ctx.run();
 }
```

The client throws and the application crashes.

```
[ RUN      ] AServerConstructWithObjectPath.NoProps
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'sdbusplus::exception::SdBusError'
  what():  sd_bus_process discard: org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs: Invalid argument
Aborted                    (core dumped) ./build/test/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path
```

So a small mistake in initializing an interface can bring down an entire application because of the exception.

And because the failure looks like it's occurring on the client side, it may not even happen in the same process or close in time to when the interface was created.

Stuff like this can easily trip up reviewers if they assume the generated server can just be default constructed.

It really depends on which interface and even introducing an object path member in the PDI can break reactors using the generated server without anyone noticing.

Since suddenly the interface goes from "safe to default-construct" to "default-constructing could crash other applications", all without even modifying a single line of code in the application itself.

