How to use it?
```
ROLE=server ./build/test/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path
...
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'sdbusplus::exception::SdBusError'
  what():  sd_bus_process discard: org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs: Invalid argument
Aborted                    (core dumped) ROLE=server ./build/test/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path
```
and client
```
ROLE=client ./build/test/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path
...
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'sdbusplus::exception::SdBusError'
  what():  method: org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.NoReply: Remote peer disconnected
Aborted                    (core dumped) ROLE=client ./build/test/test_aserver_construct_with_object_path
```

The server default initializes the object path property to "" (current buggy behavior) and then 2 days later when the client actually tries to read the property, both of them get an exception.

Now exceptions can be caught and signals can be handled, but we can prevent this class of bug entirely by

- default construction to a valid object path (focus of this patch). This would already prevent the bug.

- perhaps implementing some checks into `object_path` constructor to throw an exception when an invalid path is passed (this would also make similar bugs show up as soon as the server starts, but requires extra cycles)


> Please point to code where that happens.

You can construct such a bug with any aserver that has object path properties.
Simply use the first constructor without passing properties and you will achieve the bug.

Repeating myself here, but i can't point to any merged example where the bug happens. People test their code and are aware of these problems.

Again, working and bug-free code does not imply the underlying APIs or libraries are good design or bug free.

People have built linux on top of raw pointers and C code and yet it still works super reliable.

